# A tour of Calcium

This document is both a tutorial and a test. Every `=>` below is checked by
`cargo test`, so if the engine ever stops agreeing with it, the build fails.

Write `=>` anywhere and the answer appears to its right.

    2 + 2       => 4
    7 * 6       => 42
    2^10        => 1,024
    sqrt(2)     => 1.4142

Everything is plain text. The answers live in the document, so a file can be
mailed, committed to git, or opened in any editor.


## Arithmetic

The usual operators, with the usual precedence.

    1 + 2 * 3   => 7
    (1 + 2) * 3 => 9
    10 / 4      => 2.5
    17 mod 5    => 2
    2^32 - 1    => 4,294,967,295

Division is exact until it is printed, so no rounding creeps in along the way.

    1/3 + 1/6   => 0.5
    -22/7       => -3.1429

Numbers are grouped above a thousand, and very large or very small ones switch
to exponent notation.

    999             => 999
    1000            => 1,000
    2.5e3           => 2,500
    1.5e-3          => 0.0015
    1e20 * 3        => 3e20


## Variables

A name and a value. The name may contain spaces, which is what makes a
document readable.

    radius = 3
    area   = pi * radius^2 => 28.2743

    wheel diameter = 26 inch
    wheel circumference
      = pi * wheel diameter in m
      => 2.0747 m

Definitions are recomputed, not frozen. Change an input and everything
downstream follows.

    side   = 4
    volume = side^3 => 64

    side   = 5
    volume => 125


## Functions

Give a definition a parameter list and it becomes a function.

    to fahrenheit(c) = 9/5 * c + 32

    to fahrenheit(0)   => 32
    to fahrenheit(100) => 212

Arguments can be passed by name, in any order.

    hypotenuse(a, b) = sqrt(a^2 + b^2)

    hypotenuse(3, 4)      => 5
    hypotenuse(b = 4, a = 3) => 5

Functions may recurse.

    factorial(n) = if n <= 1 then 1 else n * factorial(n - 1)

    factorial(5)  => 120
    factorial(10) => 3,628,800

A `let` keeps the body of a longer function readable.

    quadratic mean(a, b) =
      let squares = a^2 + b^2 in
      sqrt(squares / 2)

    quadratic mean(3, 4) => 3.5355


## Units

Units are ordinary names, so they combine and cancel by algebra alone. Convert
with `in`.

    100 ft in m     => 30.48 m
    1 mile in km    => 1.6093 km
    26 inch in cm   => 66.04 cm
    1 day in s      => 86,400 s
    2 cups in floz  => 16 floz

Compound units work the same way.

    60 mph in m/s        => 26.8224 m/s
    12 V * 2 A in W      => 24 W
    100 W * 1 hour in kJ => 360 kJ

Unlike quantities stay apart until you ask for a conversion. This is on
purpose: it keeps a schedule readable instead of collapsing it into seconds.

    build time = 4 days + 3 weeks => 4 days + 3 weeks
    build time in days            => 25 days

Terms come back in a fixed order — by name, with any constant last — so a
result is the same every time it is recomputed.

SI prefixes are understood without being listed anywhere.

    1 GB in MB   => 1,000 MB
    1 GiB in MiB => 1,024 MiB
    2500 W in kW => 2.5 kW

You can invent units by defining them.

    dog = 30 lbs
    1 ton in dog => 66.6667 dog


## Percentages and bases

    15% * 80    => 12
    3/8 in %    => 37.5%
    255 in hex  => 0xFF
    255 in binary => 0b11111111
    0xFF + 1    => 0x100


## Solving

Ask for a variable that has no value and Calcium looks back through the
document for something that determines it.

    perimeter = 2 * (w + h)

    w => -h + 0.5 perimeter

That works on equations too, not just definitions.

    3x + 6 = 0
    x => -2

A quadratic gives both roots.

    y^2 - 5y + 6 = 0
    y => [2, 3]


## Lists and matrices

    [1, 2; 3, 4] + [5, 6; 7, 8] => [6, 8; 10, 12]
    [1, 2; 3, 4] * [5; 6]       => [17; 39]
    [1, 2; 3, 4]^T              => [1, 3; 2, 4]
    |[1, 2; 3, 4]|              => -2
    |[3, 4]|                    => 5

Ranges generate data, and `map`, `filter` and `reduce` work over it.

    map(x^2, 1..4)              => [1, 4, 9, 16]
    filter(x > 2, [1, 2, 3, 4]) => [3, 4]
    reduce(acc + x, [1, 2, 3, 4]) => 10
    sum(x, x = 1..5)            => 15
    prod(x, x = 1..5)           => 120

A range also stands for a measurement with error, and arithmetic carries the
uncertainty through.

    measured = (5 - 0.1)..(5 + 0.1) => 4.9..5.1
    measured^2                      => 24.01..26.01


## Calculus

    der(x^3, x)    => 3 x^2
    der(sin(x), x) => cos(x)

    position(t) = 1/2*a*t^2 + v*t

    der(position, t)    => a*t + v
    der(position, t, 2) => a


## Complex numbers

`i` is a symbol like any other, with one rule attached: `i^2` is `-1`.

    i^2                => -1
    (2 + 3i)*(2 - 3i)  => 13
    sqrt(-9)           => 3i


## Prose and calculation

Indented lines are calculations. Unindented lines that read like sentences are
prose, and are left alone. This paragraph is prose because it ends with a full
stop.

1 + 1 => 2

That line was treated as a calculation because it has no sentence punctuation
and does contain an operator.
